# converted from 08-the-last-dawn.md
@title The Last Dawn
@bg field
@mood hope

narrate He turned to the field.
corin neutral You've all lost someone to it,
corin neutral Every one of you. That's why you're standing in a field eleven miles from anywhere in the rain. And I've got a hole in the ground and a hand full of black and no idea what I'm doing — but I know what it does. It takes what you have and it never quotes a price.
narrate He held up the marked hand.
cael neutral So I'm not going to ask it for anything.
narrate He asked the field instead.
corin neutral I want a memory,
corin neutral One each. Any one. The best one you've got — the one you'd keep if you could only keep one. And I want you to give it up, on purpose, out loud, to that hole.
narrate Nobody moved.
cael neutral That's insane,
corin neutral Yes,
corin neutral It is. And it's also the arithmetic. Because the thing under this field measures a request by what it is, not by what it costs the asker — and a Fever ended by four thousand people who each gave up the best thing they had is not the same request as a Fever ended by one man who hated himself.
narrate He looked at Cael.
cael neutral And it can't be one man. It was never going to be one man. That's the thing you've been getting wrong for seventeen years.
narrate Cael stared at him.
narrate And then — for the third and last time in his life — Cael laughed.
corin neutral You absolute—
cael neutral I know.
corin neutral You absolute—
corin neutral I know,
corin neutral Sit down. You're going first.
narrate It took four hours.
narrate That was the part nobody ever believed afterwards, in the eleven years of stories that followed: that it took four hours, and that it was, for most of it, quiet.
narrate They went one at a time. Four thousand people in a fold of ground, in the rain, and one at a time they walked to the edge of the hole and said out loud the best thing they had, and gave it up.
narrate A woman said: my daughter's first word. A man said: the smell of my mother's kitchen. A boy of nine — Nessa's brother, who had been nine in the reeds and was twelve now — said: the day my sister came home.
narrate Marta Osric said: forty-one years.
narrate Iven said: the look on Tam's face when he found out we could stay.
narrate Bryn said: the spring after he was born.
narrate And Cael, last, standing at the edge with a cracked hand and nothing left to trade, said:
narrate The mark went out.
narrate Not gradually. Corin felt it happen the way you feel a lamp go out in a room you had forgotten was lit — a sudden absence, and then the discovery that you had been standing in a glow for five years and had never once noticed.
narrate He looked at his palm. Clean. The circle was gone.
narrate And the Fever went with it. All of it, everywhere, for as long as there were people to catch it — which is what he had asked for in that field five years earlier, in a voice that did not shake, and which had finally, at the cost of four thousand small beautiful things, been paid.
narrate He would not remember the bread. That one was gone for good.
narrate But he would remember this. Every second of it. That was the trade the field had made, and it was the best trade he ever made.
narrate Cael died at dawn.
narrate He had four days and he had used them, and when it was finished he sat down on the grass at the edge of a hole in a field and did not get up again, and it was not dramatic and it was not a scene.
narrate Corin sat with him.
cael neutral Did it work?
corin neutral Yes.
cael neutral All of it?
corin neutral All of it.
cael neutral Good.
cael neutral I can't hear her.
corin neutral I know.
cael neutral I've never been able to hear her.
cael neutral That's the only thing I wanted, at the end. Not the Fever. Not the forgiveness. Just — the one song. She sang, I know she sang, I can't—
narrate And Corin — who had a ledger, and who had read it back, and who had eleven entries about things he could no longer feel — did the last arithmetic of his life.
narrate He had nothing left to trade. The mark was gone.
narrate But he had four thousand people in a field, and every one of them had just given away the best thing they had, and there was a great deal of that in the air.
narrate He leaned down and put his mouth next to the old man's ear and said:
narrate Cael's eyes opened.
narrate And something happened in them that Corin would spend the rest of his life failing to describe — because he had heard it, once, in a burned kitchen, from a man who had spent seventeen years unable to say it.
narrate The old man heard his daughter's voice.
cael neutral Ah,
narrate And then he was gone.

